WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.164
The telegram that Detective Fix sent to Scotland Yard had an instant effect on opinion in London.

2
00:00:06.165 --> 00:00:13.371
Suddenly Phileas Fogg was no longer an eccentric rich man who had accepted a wager to travel around the world.

3
00:00:13.372 --> 00:00:16.840
Now he was a suspected gentleman bank robber!

4
00:00:16.841 --> 00:00:22.239
Betting on his grand adventure ceased, and his name was dropped from the betting books.

5
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:30.187
Instead his name filled the papers as speculation grew about his connection to the theft of the fifty-five-thousand pounds.

6
00:00:30.188 --> 00:00:36.408
Mr. Fogg, oblivious to the telegram or any news at home, was focused just on his goal.

7
00:00:36.409 --> 00:00:46.134
In fact, after breakfast, and while Passepartout was shopping for necessities in Suez, Mr. Fogg went to see the captain of the Mongolia

8
00:00:46.135 --> 00:00:52.455
. "My good sir," began Mr. Fogg, "may I have a word with you regarding our voyage to

9
00:00:52.456 --> 00:00:59.542
Bombay?" "I can speak with you only briefly, sir, as we are set to sail in less than an hour," said the

10
00:00:59.543 --> 00:01:03.749
captain. "What I have to say is brief," replied Mr. Fogg.

11
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:10.212
"I am in a great hurry and would be obliged if you could reach Bombay in the shortest time possible.

12
00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:17.520
In fact, I am willing to offer you a considerable sum if you arrive in Bombay more than a day ahead of schedule."

13
00:01:17.521 --> 00:01:22.941
The captain was surprised by Mr. Fogg’s request but had no trouble agreeing to make haste.

14
00:01:22.942 --> 00:01:30.418
When Passepartout returned to the Mongolia shortly before it set sail, Mr. Fogg was waiting for him.

15
00:01:30.419 --> 00:01:34.871
Detective Fix was also on board, planning Mr. Fogg’s arrest.

16
00:01:34.872 --> 00:01:39.994
The detective hoped that the warrant for Mr. Fogg would be ready when the boat arrived in Bombay.

17
00:01:39.995 --> 00:01:46.104
After passing through the famous canal at Suez, the Mongolia entered the Red Sea.

18
00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:53.903
Winds from the African coast made the waves boisterous, and many passengers speedily disappeared below deck.

19
00:01:53.904 --> 00:02:00.882
As he watched the wind and the billowing waves, was Phileas Fogg anxious about the steamer’s progress?

20
00:02:00.883 --> 00:02:07.254
As calmly as if he were sitting in his Reform Club, he passed through the Red Sea with indifference.

21
00:02:07.255 --> 00:02:12.150
When the weather calmed, he did not view the historic villages on the coast.

22
00:02:12.151 --> 00:02:18.216
The Mongolia ploughed on into the Arabian Sea, and he paid no attention.

23
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:27.599
He ate four hearty meals a day, slept soundly every night, and passed his time playing whist with other enthusiastic card players like himself.

24
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.333
Passepartout, however, thoroughly enjoyed watching the scenery.

25
00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:37.843
He spent hours every day lounging on the deck and taking in his surroundings.

26
00:02:37.844 --> 00:02:40.678
He ate his meals in the forward cabin.

27
00:02:40.679 --> 00:02:46.491
It was there one day where he spotted the obliging person with whom he had talked in Suez.

28
00:02:46.492 --> 00:02:54.937
"If I am not mistaken," Passepartout said, approaching Detective Fix, "you are the gentleman who kindly helped me in Suez."

29
00:02:54.938 --> 00:03:03.454
"Ah! I recognize you. Yes, you are the servant of the unusual Englishman," said Fix with a smile.

30
00:03:03.455 --> 00:03:06.898
"Exactly, monsieur," said Passepartout. "Fix,

31
00:03:06.899 --> 00:03:10.437
my name is Fix." "Monsieur

32
00:03:10.438 --> 00:03:17.221
Fix, I am charmed to meet you. Where are you bound?" asked Passepartout. "Like

33
00:03:17.222 --> 00:03:20.970
you, to Bombay," replied Fix. "Splendid.

34
00:03:20.971 --> 00:03:29.661
I have just finished my lunch," said Passepartout, as he reached into his pocket and pulled out his large silver watch. "It

35
00:03:29.662 --> 00:03:35.099
is now one o’clock. Would you like to walk around the deck with me?" "Yes,

36
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:42.766
I would. But your watch does not tell the right time, sir. It is slow," said Fix. "My

37
00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:46.707
watch? It is a family watch," said Passepartout. "It

38
00:03:46.708 --> 00:03:54.631
has come down to me from my great-grandfather! It doesn’t vary five minutes in a year. It’s a perfect timepiece." "I

39
00:03:54.632 --> 00:04:03.999
see," said Fix. "I believe you have kept London time, which is two hours behind that of the Suez. You

40
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.360
should regulate your watch at noon in each country." "Regulate

41
00:04:08.361 --> 00:04:12.912
my watch?" asked Passepartout. "Never!" "Well,

42
00:04:12.913 --> 00:04:16.944
then, it will not agree with the sun," said Fix. "That

43
00:04:16.945 --> 00:04:19.965
is a problem for the sun, monsieur!" And

44
00:04:19.966 --> 00:04:25.516
with a defiant gesture, Passepartout returned his watch to his pocket. Fix

45
00:04:25.517 --> 00:04:33.078
decided to change the subject and in the most casual tone asked, "Is Mr. Fogg getting on well?" "Quite

46
00:04:33.079 --> 00:04:36.451
well," said Passepartout. "And I am too. It

47
00:04:36.452 --> 00:04:40.842
must be the sea air. I am eating like a famished giant." Fix

48
00:04:40.843 --> 00:04:46.726
smiled, and their amiable conversation continued with innocent topics. During

49
00:04:46.727 --> 00:04:53.202
the crossing Fix and Passepartout got into the habit of meeting and chatting regularly. Fix

50
00:04:53.203 --> 00:05:01.387
often offered Passepartout a whiskey or beer in the steamer’s bar, and Passepartout never failed to accept his generosity. The

51
00:05:01.388 --> 00:05:08.629
Mongolia was due in Bombay on October 22. But it arrived on October 20. So

52
00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:17.057
Mr. Fogg had gained two days, which he calmly entered in his travel journal after paying the captain his reward. Fix,

53
00:05:17.058 --> 00:05:20.390
however, was not as happy. As

54
00:05:20.391 --> 00:05:28.325
soon as the ship docked, Fix hastily made his way to the local police station in search of the warrant for Fogg. But being

55
00:05:28.326 --> 00:05:37.413
two days ahead of schedule, he knew the chances of finding it waiting for him were slim. Passepartout's Guidebook

56
00:05:37.414 --> 00:05:42.147
As you just read, Mr. Fogg and I arrived in Bombay, India.

57
00:05:42.148 --> 00:05:46.188
Now you might be wondering why British police were in India.

58
00:05:46.189 --> 00:05:49.527
That's because India is a British colony.

59
00:05:49.528 --> 00:05:53.445
A colony is a country controlled by another country.

60
00:05:53.446 --> 00:05:59.045
Many powerful European countries have colonies in the Americas, Asia, and Africa.

61
00:05:59.046 --> 00:06:04.617
The main reason Britain has established colonies is so it can profit through trade.

62
00:06:04.618 --> 00:06:11.556
India has many spices, teas, and grains that are sold in Europe for six-thousand times the price they cost in India!

63
00:06:11.557 --> 00:06:17.720
In fact, India is so profitable, it's called the "jewel in the crown" of the British Empire.

64
00:06:17.721 --> 00:06:22.561
There has been a lot of resistance to British rule in India; many Indians are very poor.

65
00:06:22.562 --> 00:06:30.254
But Britain maintains its control with its very powerful army.

